A shared [schema](/lore/schemas-define-the-boundary) change needs time for consumers to move. Discover old uses and prevent new uses before removing the obsolete [interface](/lore/one-schema-three-surfaces).

> many refactorings require you to run both the original and new versions of the schema in parallel during a transition period that is long enough to allow any applications accessing that portion of the schema time to be redeployed.

Source: [Q19](/lore/durability-bibliography#q19); [B14](/lore/durability-bibliography#b14-refactoring-databases), [B15](/lore/durability-bibliography#b15-software-engineering-at-google).

## Failure it prevents

An independently deployed consumer breaks, or a migration never converges because new old-style uses keep appearing.

## Our Effect 4.0.0 reading

`Schema` decoding can express supported representations. Database migrations, consumer rollout, usage evidence, and static backsliding checks remain separate responsibilities.

Source: `packages/effect/src/Schema.ts`.

## How to check

Test old and new consumers during the transition. Prove old usage is gone before contraction. This check is our proposal. The books do not prescribe it.

Continue with [Old events must still decode](/lore/old-events-must-still-decode), [Recovery needs model tests](/lore/recovery-needs-model-tests).

Connect this to [the existing rat-stack guidance](/lore/property-based-testing).

